#   THE ASSIGNMENT 
It was to handle the missingness in the dateset of Hormonal and Biochemical Profile in Patients

#   DATA DESCRIPTION
The dataset contained identifiers (record_id), hormaonal profile (hormonr1 to hormone14), Lipid variables (lipods1 to lipids15), and categorical factors (ethnicity, PCOS, clinical outcomes)

#   ENVIRONMENT AND REQUIREMENTS
R Version: 4.5.x3 (Recommended)
Core Libraries:
tidyverse (dplyr, ggplot2, tidyr): Data manipulation and visualization.
skimr: Summary statistics.
mice: Multiple Imputation by Chained Equations.
naniar & visdat: Missing data visualization.
dbscan: Local Outlier Factor (LOF) calculation.

#   PROCEEDURE
1. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Initial screening using skim() to understand distributions.
Filtered out variables with >35% missingness to maintain data integrity.
2. Missing Data Analysis
Visualization: Used vis_miss() and gg_miss_var() to identify if data was Missing Completely at Random (MCAR).
Hypothesis Testing: Outlined Little’s MCAR test logic to determine the appropriate imputation strategy.
3. Multiple Imputation (MICE)
Two methods were compared for filling in missing values:
Predictive Mean Matching (PMM): The default for numeric data.
Random Forest (RF): Used to capture non-linear relationships between hormones and lipids.
Validation: Density plots were generated to ensure imputed distributions matched the original data shape.
4. Outlier Detection
Univariate: Standard boxplots for individual lipid variables.
Multivariate (Advanced): Implemented the Local Outlier Factor (LOF) algorithm. This identifies "hidden" outliers by comparing the local density of a point to its neighbors—catching anomalies that a standard boxplot would miss.
